  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 353 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA fast moving trough within a northwesterly flow aloft along the \nBC/Pac NW coast will slow considerably through the day today as it \nintensifies and eventually splits some of its energy. The weaker \nportion of this troughing skirts across the Canadian border this \nevening and tonight, which will foster some generally light rain \nnear and north of Highway 2. \n\nThe stronger portion of the aforementioned trough becomes cutoff \nover northern CA/vicinity Wednesday, leading to a southwesterly flow \naloft developing across the Northern Rockies. Temperatures look to \ntrend warmer as a result of this flow aloft, but given the \nanticyclonic nature of the flow typical afternoon and evening shower \nand thunderstorm activity looks to hold off til Thursday as the core \nof the upper disturbance begins its approach. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms, a few on the strong side, look to develop \nThursday afternoon, initially across Southwest Montana. Uncertainty \nincreases Thursday night into Friday with respect to the track and \ntiming of the upper disturbance, though there will be at least some \nrisk for another round of showers and thunderstorms by Friday \nafternoon. \n\nLooking toward the weekend the upper level disturbance begins to \nexit eastward. A few lingering showers early Saturday morning cannot \nbe ruled out ahead of a drying trend through the remainder of the \nweekend into early next week. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe largest source of uncertainty is with respect to the timing\nand track of the upper level disturbance Thursday into Friday. A\nslower track, or one that keeps the Northern Rockies within a\nsouthwesterly flow aloft longer would foster a second day of\nshowers and thunderstorms. The track of this disturbance will also\nplay a large role in how much shear and resulting risk for\norganized severe weather will materialize. -AM",
